two("operating-systems", "Operating Systems",
    "How an operating system works inside: processes, scheduling, memory and files.",
    "operating-systems", "intermediate", "free", 180,
    "Processes and scheduling",
    L("processes-and-threads", "Processes, threads and scheduling", "free", 14,
      "## Sharing one CPU\n\nA **process** is a running program with its own address space. A **thread** is a unit of execution inside a process, sharing its memory. The **scheduler** decides which ready thread gets the CPU next. Switching between them is a **context switch**: saving one thread's registers and loading another's, which costs time.\n\nCommon policies: **round robin** (each gets a time slice), **priority** scheduling, and multilevel queues. A process is usually *running*, *ready* or *blocked* (waiting for I/O).",
      quiz=[Q("A thread differs from a process because threads in a process:", ["Share the same memory", "Cannot run in parallel", "Always run slower", "Have no stack"], 0, "Threads share the address space; each has its own stack."),
            Q("A blocked process is waiting for:", ["An event such as I/O", "More CPU cores", "A reboot", "A new compiler"], 0, "It cannot run until the event occurs.")]),
    "Memory",
    L("virtual-memory", "Virtual memory and paging", "free", 14,
      "## Each process sees its own memory\n\nWith **virtual memory**, every process uses virtual addresses that the hardware and OS translate to physical memory using **page tables**. Memory is split into fixed-size **pages**. Benefits: isolation between processes, the ability to use more memory than physically installed (pages not in use can live on disk), and simple allocation.\n\nA **page fault** occurs when a needed page is not in RAM; the OS loads it from disk. Too many faults cause **thrashing**, where the system spends its time swapping instead of working. The **TLB** caches recent translations to keep lookups fast.",
      quiz=[Q("A page fault happens when:", ["The needed page is not currently in physical memory", "The CPU overheats", "A file is deleted", "A thread exits"], 0, "The OS must bring the page in."),
            Q("Virtual memory gives each process:", ["Isolation from other processes' memory", "Unlimited speed", "Direct access to hardware", "No need for an OS"], 0, "Translation enforces separate address spaces.")])),

two("computer-networks", "Computer Networks",
    "How data travels: layers, TCP and UDP, DNS, HTTP and TLS.",
    "networking", "intermediate", "free", 180,
    "The layers",
    L("layers-and-tcp", "Network layers and TCP", "free", 14,
      "## Layers hide complexity\n\nThe internet is built in layers: **link** (Ethernet, Wi-Fi), **internet** (IP addresses and routing), **transport** (TCP/UDP) and **application** (HTTP, DNS...). Each layer wraps the data from the one above in its own header.\n\n**TCP** gives a reliable ordered byte stream: a **three-way handshake** (SYN, SYN-ACK, ACK) opens the connection, lost segments are retransmitted, and congestion control slows the sender when the network is busy. **UDP** is lighter and does not guarantee delivery, which suits live audio, games and DNS queries.",
      quiz=[Q("Which protocol guarantees ordered, reliable delivery?", ["TCP", "UDP", "ARP", "ICMP"], 0, "TCP retransmits and orders segments."),
            Q("The TCP handshake consists of:", ["SYN, SYN-ACK, ACK", "GET, POST, PUT", "HELLO, BYE", "ACK only"], 0, "Three messages open the connection.")]),
    "Names and the web",
    L("dns-http-and-tls", "DNS, HTTP and TLS", "free", 14,
      "## From a name to a page\n\n**DNS** translates a hostname into an IP address, with answers cached at several levels (browser, operating system, resolver) for a time-to-live. The client then opens a TCP connection, performs a **TLS handshake** to authenticate the server and agree on encryption keys, and sends an **HTTP** request.\n\nHTTP/2 multiplexes many requests over one connection; HTTP/3 runs over QUIC (on UDP) to reduce delay. **CDNs** serve content from locations near users to cut latency.",
      quiz=[Q("What does DNS do?", ["Maps hostnames to IP addresses", "Encrypts traffic", "Stores web pages", "Assigns passwords"], 0, "It is the internet's name lookup."),
            Q("Why do CDNs lower latency?", ["They serve content from nearby locations", "They compress CPUs", "They remove DNS", "They block TLS"], 0, "Shorter physical distance means less delay.")])),

two("microservices", "Microservices",
    "When to split a system into services, how services talk to each other and how to keep them reliable.",
    "microservices", "intermediate", "premium", 180,
    "Foundations",
    L("monolith-or-microservices", "Monolith or microservices?", "free", 12,
      "## Start simple\n\nA **monolith** is one deployable unit. It is simpler to build, test and run, and it scales well for many teams if its modules are kept well separated.\n\n**Microservices** split a system into small services, each owning its data and deployed independently. They help large organisations move in parallel and scale parts separately, but add costs: network calls can fail or be slow, data is spread across services, and operations (deploy, monitor, trace) get harder.\n\nA sensible path: build a **modular monolith** first and extract a service only when a clear need (team independence, scaling, different technology) appears.",
      quiz=[Q("A key cost of microservices is:", ["Distributed-system complexity (network failures, data consistency)", "No ability to deploy", "No ability to scale", "Slower typing"], 0, "Calls that were function calls become network calls."),
            Q("Why consider a modular monolith first?", ["It is simpler and can be split later when needed", "Monoliths are always faster", "Services are illegal", "It needs no tests"], 0, "Avoid paying the distribution cost before you need to.")]),
    "Reliability",
    L("resilient-service-calls", "Resilient service calls", "premium", 14,
      "## Expect failure\n\nEvery remote call can be slow or fail. Defend with:\n\n- **Timeouts** on every call.\n- **Retries with backoff and jitter**, only for idempotent operations.\n- **Circuit breakers** that stop calling a failing service for a while so it can recover.\n- **Idempotency keys** so a retried request does not repeat its effect.\n- **Asynchronous messaging** for work that does not need an immediate answer.\n\nUse correlation IDs and distributed tracing so one request can be followed across services.",
      quiz=[Q("Why add jitter to retry delays?", ["So many clients do not retry at the same instant", "To make calls slower", "To skip timeouts", "To encrypt requests"], 0, "Synchronised retries can overload a recovering service."),
            Q("A circuit breaker:", ["Stops calls to a failing service for a while", "Speeds up the database", "Encrypts messages", "Removes retries"], 0, "It gives the dependency time to recover.")])),

two("apache-kafka", "Apache Kafka",
    "How Kafka stores and delivers event streams: topics, partitions, consumer groups and delivery guarantees.",
    "kafka", "intermediate", "premium", 180,
    "Core concepts",
    L("topics-partitions-and-offsets", "Topics, partitions and offsets", "free", 14,
      "## A distributed commit log\n\nKafka stores events in **topics**. Each topic is split into **partitions**, each an append-only ordered log. Every record in a partition has an **offset**, its position. Order is guaranteed **within a partition**, not across the whole topic, so records that must stay ordered (all events of one order) should share a **key**, which maps them to the same partition.\n\nPartitions are **replicated** across brokers; one replica is the leader and the others follow. Records are kept for a retention period, so consumers can re-read them.",
      quiz=[Q("In which scope does Kafka guarantee ordering?", ["Within a partition", "Across a whole topic", "Across the cluster", "Never"], 0, "Use a key to keep related events in one partition."),
            Q("An offset identifies:", ["A record's position within a partition", "A broker's address", "A consumer's name", "A topic's size"], 0, "It is the sequential position in the log.")]),
    "Consuming",
    L("consumer-groups-and-delivery", "Consumer groups and delivery guarantees", "premium", 14,
      "## Scaling consumers\n\nConsumers in the same **consumer group** share a topic's partitions: each partition is read by one consumer in the group, so adding consumers (up to the partition count) increases throughput. Different groups each receive all records independently.\n\nConsumers **commit offsets** to record progress. Committing after processing gives **at-least-once** delivery (a crash may cause reprocessing), so make processing **idempotent**. Kafka also supports **exactly-once** semantics for read-process-write flows within Kafka using transactions.",
      quiz=[Q("In one consumer group, a partition is read by:", ["Exactly one consumer at a time", "All consumers", "No one", "Only the leader broker"], 0, "That is how the group divides the work."),
            Q("Committing offsets after processing gives:", ["At-least-once delivery", "Exactly-zero delivery", "Random delivery", "No delivery"], 0, "A crash before the commit means reprocessing.")])),

two("java", "Java",
    "Java from the ground up: syntax, object-oriented basics, collections and how the JVM runs your code.",
    "java", "beginner", "free", 200,
    "Language basics",
    L("classes-and-objects", "Classes, objects and interfaces", "free", 14,
      "## Object-oriented Java\n\nJava is statically typed and object-oriented. A **class** defines state (fields) and behaviour (methods); an **object** is an instance. **Interfaces** define a contract that classes implement, which lets you program against abstractions. Since Java 16, **records** give concise immutable data carriers.",
      code=("java", "public interface Shape { double area(); }\n\nrecord Circle(double r) implements Shape {\n    public double area() { return Math.PI * r * r; }\n}\n\nrecord Rect(double w, double h) implements Shape {\n    public double area() { return w * h; }\n}", "An interface and two records"),
      quiz=[Q("An interface in Java defines:", ["A contract that classes can implement", "A database table", "A thread", "A package manager"], 0, "Callers depend on the contract, not the class."),
            Q("A Java record is mainly used for:", ["Concise immutable data holders", "Network sockets", "Garbage collection", "Build scripts"], 0, "It generates constructor, accessors, equals and hashCode.")]),
    "Collections and the JVM",
    L("collections-and-the-jvm", "Collections and the JVM", "free", 14,
      "## Data structures and runtime\n\nThe core collections: `ArrayList` (dynamic array), `LinkedList`, `HashMap` (hash table), `HashSet`, `TreeMap` (sorted) and `ArrayDeque` (stack or queue). Choose by the operations you need.\n\nJava source compiles to **bytecode** that the **JVM** runs, with a **just-in-time (JIT) compiler** optimising hot code and an automatic **garbage collector** reclaiming unreachable objects, so you do not free memory by hand.",
      code=("java", "Map<String, Integer> counts = new HashMap<>();\nfor (String w : words) {\n    counts.merge(w, 1, Integer::sum);\n}", "Counting words"),
      quiz=[Q("What does the garbage collector do?", ["Reclaims memory of unreachable objects", "Compiles code", "Opens files", "Encrypts data"], 0, "Memory is freed automatically."),
            Q("Which collection keeps keys sorted?", ["TreeMap", "HashMap", "HashSet", "ArrayDeque"], 0, "TreeMap orders by key.")])),

two("cpp", "C++",
    "C++ fundamentals: value semantics, the standard library and managing resources safely with RAII.",
    "cpp", "intermediate", "free", 200,
    "Essentials",
    L("values-references-and-the-stl", "Values, references and the standard library", "free", 14,
      "## Control with responsibility\n\nC++ gives direct control over memory and performance. Variables are **values** by default; pass by **reference** (`const T&`) to avoid copies. The **Standard Template Library (STL)** provides containers (`vector`, `map`, `unordered_map`, `set`), algorithms (`sort`, `find`) and iterators.\n\n`std::vector` is the default container: contiguous memory, amortised O(1) `push_back`, and cache-friendly iteration.",
      code=("cpp", "#include <algorithm>\n#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n\nint main() {\n    std::vector<int> v{5, 2, 9, 1};\n    std::sort(v.begin(), v.end());\n    for (int x : v) std::cout << x << ' ';\n}", "Sorting a vector"),
      quiz=[Q("Why pass large objects as `const T&`?", ["It avoids copying while preventing modification", "It makes them global", "It deletes them", "It is required for ints"], 0, "No copy, no accidental change."),
            Q("std::vector stores its elements:", ["In contiguous memory", "In a linked list", "On disk", "In a hash table"], 0, "Contiguity is why it is fast to iterate.")]),
    "Resource management",
    L("raii-and-smart-pointers", "RAII and smart pointers", "free", 14,
      "## Let destructors clean up\n\n**RAII** (resource acquisition is initialisation) ties a resource's lifetime to an object: acquire in the constructor, release in the destructor, so cleanup happens even when exceptions occur.\n\nPrefer **smart pointers** to raw `new`/`delete`: `std::unique_ptr` (sole owner) and `std::shared_ptr` (shared, reference-counted). Use `std::make_unique` to create them. Avoid owning raw pointers; they cause leaks and double frees.",
      code=("cpp", "#include <memory>\n\nstruct Conn { ~Conn() { /* close */ } };\n\nvoid run() {\n    auto c = std::make_unique<Conn>();\n    // ... use c; it is released automatically at scope end\n}", "unique_ptr releases automatically"),
      quiz=[Q("Which smart pointer expresses sole ownership?", ["unique_ptr", "shared_ptr", "weak_ptr", "raw pointer"], 0, "Only one owner at a time."),
            Q("RAII means resources are released:", ["When the owning object is destroyed", "At program start", "Never", "By the OS only"], 0, "The destructor does the cleanup.")])),

